# Subjects and education types

- **Do not show preset subject codes** (`efl`, `ffl`, etc.) in the UI. The subject field uses full localized labels via `subjectLabelLocalized` / datalist options built from `REPORT_SUBJECTS` + tenant custom names.
- **Form text → storage**: use `resolveDefaultSubjectInputToStorage` from `@/lib/subjectFormResolve` so typed labels map to ISO codes when they match a preset in any UI language; otherwise the trimmed string is stored as a custom subject (length rules in `@/lib/subjects`).
- **Storage → input display**: use `subjectFieldDisplayValueFromStored(raw, uiLang)` so codes become labels; legacy unset `efl` shows empty so the placeholder (“Define subject name”) appears.
- **Education type** is `grade_rubric_profile` / `GradeRubricProfile` from `GRADE_RUBRIC_PROFILES` in `@/lib/gradeRubricProfile`. Iterate that array for UI sections and options—when a new profile is added, subject lists and labels extend with it. Use `gradeRubricProfileDisplayLabel(t, rp)` for consistent labels.
- **Subject suggestions** are built per `GradeRubricProfile` in `@/lib/subjectOptionsByEducationType.ts` (`subjectSuggestionLabelsForRubric`). **Class settings** (`ClassWorkspace`) does **not** change education type—that is set upstream when the class is created. The class card shows **only** that profile’s suggestions (single datalist) and **only** that profile’s custom subjects in the manage dropdown (no other programme’s content).
- **Report subject override** (`ReportEditor`): render preset language `<option>`s only when `parseGradeRubricProfile(klass.grade_rubric_profile, "language") === "language"`; strip `inputs.subject_code` on load when the class is not language acquisition.
- **Custom subject rows** in tenant JSON include `name` and `rubric_profile`. Custom names are **scoped per education type**; the API persists them that way. Built-in languages are not stored as custom rows.
- **Changing education type** away from language should clear the subject field when it resolved to a **preset language code** (user-facing label), so primary/secondary classes do not keep a language-only preset.
